  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Giants held a lead through six innings, then gave it back in one. That's the game right there.",
    "what_this_means": "Logan Webb pitched well enough to win. 6.2 innings, three earned runs, five strikeouts. But the offense managed only seven hits and left runners stranded in critical moments. They converted one of four RISP situations. The bullpen was clean: Hentges, Winn, Miller, and Smith combined for 2.3 scoreless innings. None of it mattered. The game turned late. Seattle scored three in the seventh, and the Giants never recovered. Bryce Eldridge went 2-for-4, but single hits don't win 3-4 games. Webb did his job. The bats did enough to stay in it. The problem was the one inning where neither held.",
    "what_to_watch": "They had the game and lost it. That's inconsistency crystallized. Not a statement about the season, but a single-night reminder of how thin the margin is. One clean inning from the bullpen, one conversion with runners in scoring position, one moment where the lead holds. They got two of three. In a competitive division twenty games back, that's not enough.",
